# 15 Pressure and the projection step {#ch:projection}

## The continuous projection

After the acceleration event we have a provisional face velocity
$\mathbf{u}_f^\star$ that includes advection, viscosity and all forces except the
pressure gradient at the new time. We look for $p^{n+1}$ such that
$$
\mathbf{u}_f^{n+1} = \mathbf{u}_f^\star - \Delta t\,\alpha\,\nabla p^{n+1},\qquad \nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}_f^{n+1} = 0.
\qquad (15.1)
$$
Taking the divergence of the first equation and using the second gives the
variable-coefficient Poisson equation
$$
\boxed{\;\nabla\cdot\left(\alpha\,\nabla p^{n+1}\right) = \dfrac{\nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}_f^\star}{\Delta t}\;}
\qquad (15.2)
$$
with $\alpha = 1/\rho$. This is the Helmholtz--Hodge decomposition of
[section 7.6](07_cfd_concepts#sec:pressure-role) in a norm weighted by density: $\mathbf{u}_f^\star$ is split
into a divergence-free part and the gradient part $\Delta t\,\alpha\nabla p$
(figure 15.1). Because $\mathbf{u}_f^\star$ contains no pressure gradient,
$p^{n+1}$ is the full pressure, not an increment.

