## The discrete projection

Define the discrete divergence of a face field and the discrete face gradient
of a cell field:
$$
\begin{aligned}
(\mathcal{D}\mathbf{u}_f)_i &= \dfrac{1}{\Delta}\sum_{d}\left(u_{f,{i+\dfrac 12}} - u_{f,{i-\dfrac 12}}\right)_d,\\
(\mathcal{G}p)_{i-\dfrac 12}&= \dfrac{p_i - p_{i-1}}{\Delta}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The face velocity already contains the open-area factor $f_m$, so
$u_f\,\Delta^{d-1}$ is the volume flux through the face and $\mathcal{D}$ is a
finite-volume divergence. Then (15.2) becomes
$$
\sum_d \dfrac{\alpha_{i+\dfrac 12}(p_{i+1} - p_i) - \alpha_{i-\dfrac 12}(p_i - p_{i-1})}{\Delta^2}
= \dfrac{(\mathcal{D}\mathbf{u}_f^\star)_i}{\Delta t},
\qquad (15.5)
$$
a 5-point (2D) or 7-point (3D) Laplacian weighted by the face values of
$\alpha$. It is solved by multigrid ([chapter 16](16_multigrid)), then each face is
corrected:
$$
u_{f,{i-\dfrac 12}}^{n+1} = u_{f,{i-\dfrac 12}}^\star - \Delta t\,\alpha_{i-\dfrac 12}\,(\mathcal{G}p^{n+1})_{i-\dfrac 12}.
$$

**The new face velocity is divergence-free to solver tolerance.**
The discrete Laplacian in (15.5) is exactly
$\mathcal{D}(\alpha\mathcal{G}p)$, so
$$
\mathcal{D}\mathbf{u}_f^{n+1} = \mathcal{D}\mathbf{u}_f^\star - \Delta t\,\mathcal{D}(\alpha\mathcal{G}p^{n+1})
= \Delta t\left[\dfrac{\mathcal{D}\mathbf{u}_f^\star}{\Delta t} - \mathcal{D}(\alpha\mathcal{G}p^{n+1})\right]
= \Delta t\cdot\text{residual}.
$$
If the Poisson equation were solved exactly, the face velocity would be
exactly divergence-free. This is why VOF transport, which uses $\mathbf{u}_f$, can
conserve volume to round-off ([section 17.4](17_vof#sec:wy)).

**No checkerboard.** The face gradient $(p_i - p_{i-1})/\Delta$ is
compact, so the Laplacian in (15.5) sees every cell, and an
alternating pressure gives a large, not zero, Laplacian. The problem of
[section 7.7](07_cfd_concepts#sec:checker) does not arise.

> **In the code: `poisson.h`, `project()`**
>
> ~~~literatec
> foreach() {
>   div[] = 0.;
>   foreach_dimension()
>     div[] += uf.x[1] - uf.x[];
>   div[] /= dt*Delta;
> }
> mgstats mgp = poisson (p, div, alpha, tolerance = TOLERANCE/sq(dt), nrelax = nrelax);
> foreach_face()
>   uf.x[] -= dt*alpha.x[]*face_gradient_x (p, 0);
> ~~~

## What the tolerance means

`TOLERANCE` (default $10^{-3}$) is passed as `TOLERANCE/sq(dt)`.
From the identity above, $|\mathcal{D}\mathbf{u}_f^{n+1}|\,\Delta t= \Delta t^2\,|\text{residual}|$,
so stopping when the residual is below $\text{TOLERANCE}/\Delta t^2$ means
$$
\max_i\left|\mathcal{D}\mathbf{u}_f^{n+1}\right|_i\Delta t< \text{TOLERANCE}:
$$
no cell changes its volume by more than $0.1\%$ in one step because of
incomplete convergence. The solver prints a warning when it reaches
`NITERMAX = 100` cycles without meeting this.

## Variable density and the density ratio

The face coefficient is $\alpha_{i-\dfrac 12}= f_{m,{i-\dfrac 12}}/\rho(\bar f_{i-\dfrac 12})$, with
$\bar f$ the average of the two cell values. Across a water--air interface it
jumps from $1$ to $1/0.001225 \approx 816$. A Poisson equation with a
coefficient that jumps by three orders of magnitude over one cell is much
harder to solve than the constant-coefficient case; you see it as larger
`mgp.i` counts when steep crests are present.

## The approximate projection: updating the cell velocity {#sec:approx-proj}

The cell-centred velocity still has to be corrected. `centered.h` forms
on each face the total acceleration, force plus pressure gradient,
$$
g_{f,{i-\dfrac 12}} = f_{m,{i-\dfrac 12}}\,a_{i-\dfrac 12}- \alpha_{i-\dfrac 12}\,(\mathcal{G}p^{n+1})_{i-\dfrac 12},
$$
averages the two faces of each cell,
$$
\mathbf{g}_i = \dfrac{g_{f,{i-\dfrac 12}} + g_{f,{i+\dfrac 12}}}{f_{m,{i-\dfrac 12}} + f_{m,{i+\dfrac 12}}},
\qquad (15.9)
$$
and updates $\mathbf{u}^{n+1} = \mathbf{u}^{\star\star} + \Delta t\,\mathbf{g}$. This makes
$\mathbf{u}$ consistent with $\mathbf{u}_f$ but not exactly divergence-free in the discrete
sense: the average of two face gradients is not the gradient that the
Poisson equation used. The error is second order in $\Delta$. The method is
called an *approximate projection*. It does not harm volume
conservation, because the field used to move $f$ and momentum is always the
exactly projected $\mathbf{u}_f$, which is rebuilt from $\mathbf{u}$ and projected again
every step.

The same $\mathbf{g}$ is reused at the next step: added before the viscous
solve ([chapter 13](13_viscosity)) and used as the source term in BCG prediction.

> **In the code: `centered.h`, `centered_gradient()` and event `projection`**
>
> ~~~literatec
> gf.x[] = fm.x[]*a.x[] - alpha.x[]*(p[] - p[-1])/Delta;
> g.x[] = (gf.x[] + gf.x[1])/(fm.x[] + fm.x[1] + SEPS);
> ...
> mgp = project (uf, p, alpha, dt, mgp.nrelax);
> centered_gradient (p, g);
> correction (dt);        // u.x[] += dt*g.x[]
> ~~~

## Pressure boundary conditions {#sec:pbc}

At a wall the normal face velocity must be zero after projection. The ghost
value of the normal velocity is antisymmetric ([chapter 18](18_boundary)), so
$\overline{u} = 0$ on the wall face and $u_f^\star = \Delta t\,f_m a$ there. Setting
$u_f^{n+1} = 0$ in (15.1) gives
$$
\left.\dfrac{\partial p}{\partial n}\right|_{\text{wall}} = \dfrac{f_m\,a_n}{\alpha}:
$$
the pressure gradient at the wall must balance the force pushing into it.
This is the default pressure condition on every domain side in
`centered.h`:

~~~literatec
#define neumann_pressure(i) (a.n[i]*fm.n[i]/alpha.n[i])
p[right] = neumann (neumann_pressure(ghost));
p[left]  = neumann (- neumann_pressure(0));
~~~

The sign differs between left and right because the outward normal points in
opposite directions.

At an outflow, a Dirichlet condition such as `p[right] = dirichlet(0)`
fixes the level of $p$ and lets fluid leave freely. With all-Neumann
boundaries, (15.5) has a solution only if the sum of the
right-hand side over the domain is zero (net volume flux through the boundary
is zero), and $p$ is then defined up to a constant. The multigrid statistics
store this sum in `mgp.sum` for checking.

